ke sure  that each of them is safe each of them  has the vaccinations they need and most  importantly our veteran patients are  safe that's what this is all about so  i'm focused right here on us i'm sure  others are making these kinds of  decisions and thinking this through our  experience is that this is complicated  but it's very manageable it's leading to  an increase in vaccinations and we think  that's going to improve outcomes for our  veterans but the fact you guys decided  to come out and talk about it talk about  this positive experience the increase in  vaccinations some might read that as a  suggestion that you're laying the  groundwork for a broader mandate  well some may but i i'm not big in kind  of signaling i'm big big into  communicating very clearly and my  communication here is very clearly this  the most important thing each of us can  do whether you work at the va or work  somewhere else  to protect our veterans these heroes who  have given so much to our country the  most important thing you can do is get  yourself vaccinated that will protect  our veterans who have complicated health  care situations who have more  comorbidities because of the great  sacrifices they've made on behalf of  this country so if you want to protect  veterans  go get vaccinated that's the very simple  message i want to send mr secretary i  was surprised and also disappointed to  learn in preparing for this segment that  the typical va hospital is almost 50  years older than the typical private  hospital in this country and yet funding  to modernize those hospitals was left  out of the recent infrastructure bill  how will that impact your ability to  provide care  look first of all the most important  thing we have in these facilities are  the people and they're an unbelievable  team we're communicating with them all  day yesterday all day today about this  new vaccine requirement and that's going  to continue to be the strength of va our  people secondly  it was not